{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=9-\\frac{2}{3}x$ and the point $P(3,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(3,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =9-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(3,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a648ab828e2f48069d87e8219def2436ba09debad65a62452aba8b1413b68ff0"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+3}{5}$ and the point $P(2,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a648ab828e2f48069d87e8219def2436ba09debad65a62452aba8b1413b68ff0"} {"question" "q-15646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-15646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-75987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{2x+1}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-75987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{2x+1}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x+1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+1}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-75987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a648ab828e2f48069d87e8219def2436ba09debad65a62452aba8b1413b68ff0"} {"question" "q-75987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-75987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-75987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $-1$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(-1)=-2$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a648ab828e2f48069d87e8219def2436ba09debad65a62452aba8b1413b68ff0"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342589" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $0\\le x<2,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(-1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(-1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(-1)=x.$ For $x\\ne 2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(-1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2}\\cdot (x-2) & =-1\\cdot (x-2) \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\cancel{(x-2)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x-2)} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $0\\le x<2,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(-1)=1.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4x}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}-4(1)}{(1-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342609" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a648ab828e2f48069d87e8219def2436ba09debad65a62452aba8b1413b68ff0"} {"question" "q-342609" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"} {"question" "q-342609" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13987350/state.json" "file_sha256" "4d39f683e81798915d2b9b77f862239ad670793a1c68b53f1d68b8b0264efab2"}] "usage" {} "basis" 932 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
